What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/abi
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) Version of the diag interface: "3" for the attributes
		and values described in this file. Version 2 added
		sensor_attach and restore; version 1 had neither. Version 3
		adds the policy snapshot.

		Within a version, attributes are only added, and an existing
		attribute keeps its values and their meaning. A reader tests
		for an attribute it needs rather than for a version: a
		version 2 kernel may lack xart_guard, xart_guard_list,
		xart_guard_refusals and matching, which came later.

		The diag directory holds passive diagnostics of the Apple
		SEP (Secure Enclave Processor) driver, apple_sep, for
		unattended test tools that need to know how far the
		bring-up got without reading the kernel log. It exists while
		the driver is bound to the SEP: it is created at probe and
		removed at unbind. Every attribute in it is read-only and
		reads as ASCII followed by a newline. The policy snapshot
		contains space-separated key=value tokens.

		Each value is the state the driver recorded when it made the
		corresponding decision. Reading an attribute sends nothing
		to the SEP, waits for nothing and starts nothing. No value
		identifies a user, an enrolled finger or a key.

		Touch ID is activated by the first open of /dev/sep-bio, not
		by the boot-time bring-up, so the sensor and touchid
		attributes only move on once something has opened it.

		This deployment uses the existing T8122 profile and v4
		/dev/sep-bio interface. It does not add a new sensor protocol.
		OS firmware and system firmware are separate: qualify both.

		For an existing bag, select xart_writes=1, provision_keybag=0,
		xart_guard=1 and xarm_trace=1. trusted_keys=0 and
		filevault_api=0 omit the optional kernel APIs; both default
		to 1 to preserve existing deployments. There is no boot_uuid
		parameter: the OS identity comes from /chosen/apfs-preboot-uuid.
		Writable J613 26.6.2 attachment rejects a missing DT identity,
		explicit UUID override or disabled guard before opening xART.
		Host-store or identity-index read failure refuses attachment.

		Preserve the current keybag, host store, reference key, three
		Catacombs, ownership journal and fprint records. Different
		Linux roots must share the same current files globally, before
		module load; the kernel opens them from init_task's root.
		Never replace them with a snapshot or enrol again to migrate.
		Readiness diagnostics do not prove a successful finger match.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/profile
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) The platform profile the driver selected for this
		machine, as SoC and board, for example "T6020/J414s". Fixed
		at probe.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/boot
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) How the driver hands its shared memory to the SEP, as
		the platform profile sets it. Fixed at probe.

		====	=====================================================
		cold	The driver boots the SEP and hands the memory over
			during that boot.
		warm	The boot firmware left the SEP running, and the
			driver registers the memory with it in one message.
		====	=====================================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/protocol
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) The key-store protocol the driver speaks to the SEP, as
		the platform profile sets it. It is not read from the SEP,
		and it does not identify a sepOS build. Fixed at probe.

		========	=============================================
		sepos13		The sepOS 13.5 key store, whose endpoint the
				driver initialises before first use.
		variant5	The newer key store, which creates keybags
				with the variant-5 request.
		========	=============================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/xart
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) Whether the SEP's writes to the shared xART store reach
		the disk. Fixed at probe, when the driver opens the store.

		========	=============================================
		enabled		Writes reach the store (xart_writes=1, and
				the store validated for writing).
		disabled	Writes are refused. Touch ID enrol and verify
				are refused too, since the SEP must save
				state after each.
		========	=============================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/attach
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) Whether the SEP answered the driver's attach.

		========	=============================================
		pending		No outcome yet.
		attached	The SEP advertised at least one endpoint.
		failed		The SEP advertised no endpoint; the xART
				exchange and the key store cannot run this
				boot.
		========	=============================================

		The driver decides once about three seconds have passed
		without an endpoint and the SEP has gone quiet. With boot
		"warm" that wait starts at probe, so a SEP that never answers
		ends in "failed". With boot "cold" it starts at the first
		message from the SEP, so a SEP that never answers the boot
		request leaves this at "pending" for the rest of the boot.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/endpoints
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) The number of distinct endpoints the SEP has
		advertised, as an unsigned decimal. It reads "0" until
		attach reads "attached". Discovery goes on after the attach,
		so the number can still grow afterwards, and can exceed the
		count the attach message in the kernel log reports.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/keystore
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) Whether the boot-time bring-up opened the key store.

		=======	==============================================
		unknown	The bring-up has not decided yet.
		open	The key-store endpoint is enabled and, where the
			protocol needs it, initialised.
		closed	The bring-up ended without opening it. Keybag and
			Touch ID operations are unavailable this boot.
		=======	==============================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/keybag
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) The identity keybag that Touch ID uses.

		=======	==============================================
		unknown	Not determined yet.
		present	An identity keybag exists: one was already
			persisted, or the bring-up created one. This does
			not say that it loads; Touch ID activation loads
			it, and changes this to "failed" if that fails.
		missing	None is persisted and none was provisioned.
		failed	Provisioning, reading or loading it failed.
		=======	==============================================

		With provision_keybag=1, the default, the boot-time bring-up
		decides it once the key store is open, and it stays
		"unknown" if the key store never opens. With
		provision_keybag=0, Touch ID activation decides it when it
		reads the keybag, and it stays "unknown" if activation fails
		before that point.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/sensor
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) The fingerprint sensor on its SPI bus. The driver
		creates the sensor device when Touch ID is activated, and
		brings the sensor up then and again before each enrol or
		verify capture.

		=======	==============================================
		unbound	No SPI sensor device is bound.
		bound	The sensor is bound and has not been brought up
			since it bound.
		online	The most recent bring-up succeeded.
		failed	The most recent bring-up failed.
		=======	==============================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/touchid
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) Whether the Touch ID sensor side is ready to enrol and
		verify. Whether the enclave may save the state a match
		writes, which the xART guard can refuse, is in matching.

		=========	============================================
		unknown		Still in progress: the boot-time bring-up has
				not finished, or /dev/sep-bio is published
				but has not been opened yet, or activation
				is still running.
		ready		/dev/sep-bio is published, Touch ID was
				activated, and the most recent sensor
				bring-up succeeded.
		not-ready	Touch ID was activated, but the sensor is not
				online: it is not bound, has not been brought
				up since it bound, or its most recent
				bring-up failed. A later bring-up, which
				each enrol and verify runs, can still make
				it ready.
		failed		Touch ID cannot work this boot: the bring-up
				ended without publishing /dev/sep-bio, or
				activation failed. Neither is retried before
				the next boot.
		=========	============================================

		"failed" can be intentional. With provision_keybag=0, as on
		a read-only install (xart_writes=0, provision_keybag=0), the
		bring-up still publishes /dev/sep-bio when no identity
		keybag exists yet, and its first open then fails activation
		because there is none: keybag reads "missing" and touchid
		reads "failed". Read touchid "failed" together with keybag
		"missing" (and, on a read-only install, xart "disabled") as
		not provisioned rather than broken. Every other way to reach
		"failed" is a real failure.

		Because the first open of /dev/sep-bio activates Touch ID, a
		tool waiting for "ready" has to open it, or let its client
		do so, first. Enrol and verify also need xart to read
		"enabled".

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/sensor_attach
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) How far the automatic attach of the fingerprint sensor
		got. Touch ID activation brings the sensor up, with spaced
		retries, and if that falls short one more round follows
		about 10 s later, once the boot-time bring-up is over.

		=========	============================================
		unknown		Touch ID has not been activated, or its
				attach has not decided yet.
		attached	The sensor came online and the enclave's
				identity view was proven; restore says what
				became of the enrolled fingerprints.
		retrying	The attach-time bring-up fell short and the
				later round has still to run.
		not-ready	Every automatic bring-up fell short. Capture
				is unavailable until an enrol or verify
				brings the sensor up.
		=========	============================================

		touchid follows every later bring-up, while this attribute
		describes the attach alone.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/restore
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) What became of the enrolled fingerprints when the sensor
		was first attached this boot. Decided once, by the first
		bring-up that reaches the enclave's identity view.

		========	============================================
		unknown		No bring-up has reached the identity view.
		restored	The enclave lists the enrolled fingerprints
				and their restore was proven; the driver
				logged "Touch ID: restored N".
		empty		The enclave lists no enrolled fingerprint.
				Touch ID can enrol, and has nothing to match.
		unproven	The enclave lists fingerprints, but their
				restore could not be proven. Matching is
				refused this boot.
		========	============================================

		A tool deciding whether Touch ID is ready to match at boot
		reads touchid "ready", sensor_attach "attached", restore
		"restored" (or "empty", with nothing to match) and matching
		(not "blocked").

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/xart_guard
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.2
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) The level of the xART guard, which keeps the enclave's
		writes to the shared gigalocker away from session records
		this Linux install did not create, as an unsigned decimal.
		It is the effective level, after the xart_guard parameter's
		default (-1) has taken the platform's. Fixed at probe.

		==	================================================
		0	No guard runs: xart_guard=0, the platform's
			default is 0, or xART writes are disabled (see
			xart).
		1	Writes and deletes of session records that
			existed at probe and are not on Linux's list of
			its own records are refused.
		2	As 1, and writes to the shared root records are
			refused too.
		==	================================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/xart_guard_list
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.2
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) Whether the guard loaded Linux's list of the session
		records it created (/var/lib/aurora-sep-xart-owned.bin).
		Fixed at probe.

		===========	=========================================
		none		No guard runs (xart_guard reads "0").
		loaded		The list was read. An empty list is
				"loaded" too, and does not show that Touch
				ID was set up without the guard: a keybag
				created under it may write no record, and a
				rollback of the root file system can empty
				the list.
		unavailable	The list could not be opened or read. Every
				session record that existed at probe is
				protected and no new one can be adopted, so
				every session write is refused.
		===========	=========================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/xart_guard_refusals
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.2
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) How many of the enclave's xART writes and deletes the
		guard has refused since probe, as an unsigned decimal. A
		new record that could not be added to Linux's list counts
		too, since its write is refused. Only the first few
		refusals are logged; this counts them all.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/matching
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.2
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) Whether a fingerprint match can save the state it
		writes to xART, as far as the driver can tell. A match
		rewrites session records that existed before it, so the xART
		guard can refuse it, and the verify then fails although
		the fingerprints were restored.

		==========	==========================================
		usable		xART writes are enabled and no guard runs.
		unverified	A guard runs with its list loaded on a
				warm-attach Mac (boot "warm") and has refused
				nothing yet. Whether every record a match
				rewrites is on the list is not known until a
				match is tried.
		blocked		A match's write is refused or known to be:
				xART writes are disabled (xart "disabled"),
				the guard has refused a write
				(xart_guard_refusals above 0), the guard
				has no list (xart_guard_list
				"unavailable"), or a guard runs on a
				cold-boot Mac (boot "cold"). On a cold-boot
				Mac, creating the keybag needs xart_guard=0,
				so the records a match rewrites are not on
				the list.
		==========	==========================================

		For the guard cases the documented opt-out is xart_guard=0,
		which lets the enclave rewrite session records that another
		OS on the Mac may have created; see
		tools/aurora-sep/XART-SAFETY.md. During a boot the value
		can only move from "unverified" to "blocked", once the guard
		refuses a write.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/policy
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.2
Contact:	Satya Benson <satya@benson.earth>
Description:
		(RO, ABI 3) A space-separated key=value snapshot of the
		driver policy selected at probe: xart_writes (effective store
		writability), provision_keybag, xart_guard (effective level),
		trusted_keys, filevault_api, xarm_trace, refkey_v2 and
		identity_override (0 or 1). API flags describe the selected
		policy, not successful registration if that API later fails.

		identity_source is pending before the driver selects it, dt
		for /chosen/apfs-preboot-uuid, override for explicit parameters,
		or missing. It is updated at the actual identity decision.
		No UUID value, state, identity or key bytes are exposed.
		Reads only use cached facts and never contact the enclave.
		Module-parameter sysfs files need not exist; their absence
		is not evidence of a mismatched policy. Older diagnostic
		ABIs retain xart/xart_guard and kernel decision messages.
